# Constraint-driven architectural design

Follow the mandatory [common AI-assisted development governance protocol](../../docs/engineering/AI_ASSISTED_DEVELOPMENT_GOVERNANCE.md#24-mandatory-constraint-driven-architectural-design) for Cursor, ChatGPT, Codex and other coding agents.

- Identify requirements, applicable invariants, valid and invalid states, trust boundaries, legitimate variation and affected consumers before materially changing a design.
- Reuse canonical domain types and ports. Prefer compile-time enforcement, validated domain types and explicit state models over ambiguous primitive values or untyped escape hatches. Validate external/persisted inputs and preserve documented contracts.
- Prefer cohesive narrow interfaces, least-authority capabilities, encapsulation, immutable values and controlled state transitions where justified. Do not confuse strong typing with authorization, runtime validity or concurrency safety.
- Introduce extensibility only for demonstrated requirements or established seams; avoid speculative abstractions, gratuitous wrappers, interface fragmentation and bypasses of mandatory controls.
- Prefer structural prevention and existing executable enforcement. Verify valid-use and prohibited-use cases, relevant boundary bypasses and affected consumers; report actual evidence and remaining gaps.
- Strengthen existing designs incrementally within authorized scope. Preserve the shared-component impact, parallel coordination, root-cause remediation and test-integrity rules. No mass retyping, incompatible contract changes, new enforcement infrastructure, comprehensive master CI or automatic merges without appropriate authorization.

The canonical governance document defines the full procedure. This is mandatory design guidance, not proof of executable enforcement.
